### What is the problem?

Given an array of package weights in their required loading order and an integer D representing the number of available days, find the minimum ship capacity such that all packages can be shipped within D days. Packages must be shipped in the given order and cannot be split.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class ShipCapacity {

    public static int minCapacity(int[] weights, int days) {
        int low = 0, high = 0;
        for (int w : weights) {
            low = Math.max(low, w);  // must hold the heaviest package
            high += w;               // could ship everything in one day
        }

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (canShip(weights, mid, days)) {
                high = mid;          // try a smaller capacity
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;       // need more capacity
            }
        }
        return low;
    }

    private static boolean canShip(int[] weights, int capacity, int maxDays) {
        int daysUsed = 1;
        int currentLoad = 0;
        for (int w : weights) {
            if (currentLoad + w > capacity) {
                daysUsed++;
                currentLoad = 0;
            }
            currentLoad += w;
        }
        return daysUsed <= maxDays;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] weights = {10000, 20000, 30000, 15000, 25000, 10000};
        int days = 3;
        System.out.println("Min capacity: " + minCapacity(weights, days));
    }
}
```

```text
Min capacity: 40000
```

### Why is this a binary-search-on-the-answer problem?

Instead of searching a sorted data array for a target, we search the range of possible capacity values for the minimum one that satisfies the constraint. The key insight is monotonicity: if a capacity C is feasible (ships within D days), then any capacity larger than C is also feasible. This creates a clean boundary between infeasible and feasible values that binary search can locate.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class MonotonicProperty {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] weights = {10000, 20000, 30000, 15000, 25000, 10000};
        System.out.println("Capacity -> Days needed:");
        for (int cap = 30000; cap <= 110000; cap += 10000) {
            int days = countDays(weights, cap);
            System.out.println("  " + cap + " -> " + days + " days"
                + (days <= 3 ? " (feasible)" : " (infeasible)"));
        }
    }

    static int countDays(int[] weights, int capacity) {
        int days = 1, load = 0;
        for (int w : weights) {
            if (load + w > capacity) { days++; load = 0; }
            load += w;
        }
        return days;
    }
}
```

```text
Capacity -> Days needed:
  30000 -> 5 days (infeasible)
  40000 -> 3 days (feasible)
  50000 -> 3 days (feasible)
  60000 -> 2 days (feasible)
  70000 -> 2 days (feasible)
  80000 -> 2 days (feasible)
  90000 -> 2 days (feasible)
  100000 -> 2 days (feasible)
  110000 -> 1 days (feasible)
```

### How does the feasibility function work?

The greedy simulation walks the weights left to right, accumulating a running daily load. When adding the next weight would exceed the current capacity, it closes the current day and starts a new one. The total days used is compared to D.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class FeasibilityTrace {

    public static boolean canShipTrace(int[] weights, int capacity, int maxDays) {
        int days = 1, load = 0;
        System.out.println("Simulating capacity=" + capacity + ":");
        for (int i = 0; i < weights.length; i++) {
            if (load + weights[i] > capacity) {
                System.out.println("  Day " + days + ": load=" + load);
                days++;
                load = 0;
            }
            load += weights[i];
        }
        System.out.println("  Day " + days + ": load=" + load);
        System.out.println("  Total days: " + days
            + (days <= maxDays ? " FEASIBLE" : " INFEASIBLE"));
        return days <= maxDays;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] weights = {10000, 20000, 30000, 15000, 25000, 10000};
        canShipTrace(weights, 40000, 3);
    }
}
```

```text
Simulating capacity=40000:
  Day 1: load=30000
  Day 2: load=30000
  Day 3: load=35000
  Total days: 3 FEASIBLE
```

### Why must the lower bound be the maximum weight?

The minimum possible capacity must be at least as large as the heaviest single package. If capacity is smaller than any package, that package can never ship, regardless of how many days are available. Setting `low = max(weights)` avoids testing impossible values.

```java
package com.bank.search;

public class LowerBoundImportance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] weights = {10000, 20000, 30000, 15000, 25000};
        int maxWeight = 0;
        for (int w : weights) maxWeight = Math.max(maxWeight, w);
        System.out.println("Max single weight: " + maxWeight);
        System.out.println("Lower bound must be >= " + maxWeight);
        System.out.println("Capacity 20000 can ship weight 30000? No!");
    }
}
```

```text
Max single weight: 30000
Lower bound must be >= 30000
Capacity 20000 can ship weight 30000? No!
```

### How is this different from Koko Eating Bananas?

Both are binary-search-on-the-answer problems, but the feasibility check differs. In Koko, each pile is independent and can be processed in ceiling-division time. In the shipping problem, packages must stay in order and cannot be split, so the feasibility check is a greedy left-to-right simulation rather than a simple sum.

| Feature | Koko Eating Bananas | Ship Packages |
|---|---|---|
| Search space | Eating speed | Ship capacity |
| Feasibility | Sum of ceil(pile/speed) | Greedy day simulation |
| Order constraint | None (piles independent) | Must ship in order |
| Lower bound | 1 | max(weights) |
| Upper bound | max(piles) | sum(weights) |

### How do you apply this to a banking settlement scenario?

At the National Bank of India, an ordered queue of payment obligations must be settled within a fixed number of business days. Obligations must settle strictly in order and cannot be split. Minimizing the daily capacity directly minimizes the liquidity the bank must reserve.

```java
package com.bank.search;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SettlementCapacity {

    public static long minDailyLimit(long[] obligations, int businessDays) {
        long low = 0, high = 0;
        for (long o : obligations) {
            low = Math.max(low, o);
            high += o;
        }
        while (low < high) {
            long mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (canSettle(obligations, mid, businessDays)) {
                high = mid;
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return low;
    }

    private static boolean canSettle(long[] obligations, long limit, int maxDays) {
        int days = 1;
        long load = 0;
        for (long o : obligations) {
            if (load + o > limit) {
                days++;
                load = 0;
            }
            load += o;
        }
        return days <= maxDays;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] obligations = {500_000, 300_000, 800_000, 200_000, 600_000, 400_000};
        int businessDays = 3;
        long result = minDailyLimit(obligations, businessDays);
        System.out.println("Minimum daily settlement limit: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Minimum daily settlement limit: 1100000
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time | O(n log S) where S = sum of weights |
| Space | O(1) |
| Binary search iterations | O(log S) |
| Each feasibility check | O(n) |

### What are common mistakes?

The two most common mistakes are:
1. Setting the lower bound to 1 instead of `max(weights)`, which leads to impossible capacity values being tested.
2. Forgetting the order constraint and trying to sort or rearrange packages, which violates the problem statement.
